<!--
    kuna castwiden (P9, sub-stage cast-policy; option castwiden off|on|literal).

    A 64-bit widening that C's own conversions perform keeps no cast.  The
    ported CastStrategyC::isExtensionCastImplied hides an INT_SEXT/INT_ZEXT only
    beside an explicit variable of the same metatype, so a widened int beside a
    loaded long, into a store through a long pointer, or times a literal prints
    its (int8) although C converts it to that same type by itself.

    gcc -O0 -fno-pie of (the bytes below are .text):

      add_load(p, i)     return p[1] + i;                 (long *p, int i)
      mul_two(a, b)      return (long)a * b;
      lit_mul(i)         return (long)i * 12 + 7;
      store_long(p, i)   p[1] = i;
      assign_size(i, o)  unsigned long n = i; o[0] = n; return n / 3;
      keep_less(p, i)    return p[0] < i;
      keep_mixed(p, c)   return (unsigned long)c[0] + (unsigned long)p[0];

    Pass 1 (castwiden off) prints every widening (#1 #3 #5 #7 #9 #11).  Pass 2
    (on) leaves out the widening beside an operand printed with the cast's type
    (#2: the loaded int8; #4: of two widened factors the right one, so the
    product stays 64-bit) and into a destination of the cast's width (#8, #10:
    a store through an int8 or uint8 pointer).  Pass 3 (literal) also prints the
    8-byte literal beside a widening with its width's suffix, so C converts the
    other operand itself (#6: 0xcL; #12: 3UL beside a (uint8) of an int4, whose
    implicit conversion is that same sign-extension).  Every pass keeps the
    widening in a comparison (#13) and a zero-extension beside a signed int8,
    where the bare operand would make the sum signed (#14).
-->
